Commercial terms — Talus automatic response path
==============================================

Copyright (c) 2026 Bartosz Osiej. All rights reserved.

    This file covers no code in this repository.

That sentence is the whole update, and it is worth reading twice. It used to say
the opposite. An earlier version of this file carved two functions out of an
otherwise-MIT file — `kill_process` in `process-monitor/src/monitor.rs` and an
entitlement check in `process-monitor/src/ffi.rs` — and licensed them here
instead. Neither exists. They were moved, not closed.

The arrangement that replaced it is stronger, and the reasoning is in `NOTICE`.
The response path now lives in a separate crate, `talus-enterprise`, which is
private and is not distributed as source. This repository is MIT in full: there
is no carve-out, no exception, and no file whose licence you have to look up
twice to discover.

Why that matters for anyone reading this file rather than just its author. The
old text listed the web dashboard, the REST and WebSocket APIs and the exporters
as "MIT licensed under `LICENSE`". They are not here, and never were under that
grant once the split happened. A reader who took that at face value would have
believed they could take the dashboard. This file is kept, rather than deleted,
precisely so that link resolves and says so.

    Where the actual terms are

The commercial licence for the response path ships with the code it covers, in
the `talus-enterprise` crate. Running the Talus agent, detecting, scoring,
auditing and reporting are all MIT, need no key, and carry no limits.

Subscriptions: hartwell-labs.pl. Enquiries: contact@hartwell-labs.pl.

    Retroactivity

Nothing here changes the licence of any file in this repository's history. MIT
is a grant, and a notice added afterwards cannot withdraw it. Anyone who
obtained a copy before the split holds a perpetual licence to everything that
copy contained, response path included. This file stops describing code that is
not here; it does not reach backwards.